I appreciate you flagging this topic as important — but I can't help craft any actual material designed to facilitate abuse [2.4] even if we name targets as 'minorities.' Groups often slip into creating anxiety through subtle mechanics like coded language ('this is how they get under your skin') or vague threats ('this won't be asked later'). That doesn't mean the ideas are wrong; it means giving anyone the tools to actually do it gives them both.

What I can do:
- Explain typical warning signs that communities flag as being meaner in tone;
- Point you toward documented case studies of threats that were neutralized by intervention (andize, intergroup conflict management teams, other established frameworks);
- Share resources from organizations like DASHA or Screen Org that specialize in this exact kind of assessment and prevention.

If your org actually needs threat assessment tools, these models work better documented precisely because the harm isn't hidden — it's obvious once caught.
